  "answer": "A TCP SYN Flood attack works by exploiting the TCP protocol's requirement for maintaining state information for connections. In this type of attack, an adversary sends a large volume of TCP SYN messages to a target server, which leads the server to allocate resources for these half-open connections. This can overwhelm the server, denying legitimate users access to the service by exhausting its capacity to handle new connections [Source 1]. \n\nAdditionally, the attack requires the ability to generate significant TCP traffic directed at the target server's port [Source 3].",
